ing that people have talked about  for a long time already luckily it  hasn't been necessary so far  and then of course we're going to run  into all the same problems that we've  run into before which means we're going  to have to produce  massive amounts of a vaccine in a short  time period and from what i've seen so  far we're not going to be able to  distribute it equitably across the globe  unless we really redouble our efforts on  that front as well  now omicron is the latest in a string of  coronavirus mutations people are tired  of the ongoing restrictions how many  more um mutations are we likely to see  is there any any light at the end of the  corona tunnel right now  i mean  that that's of course someone nobody can  can tell right at the moment um what we  have seen this virus has been surprising  in just how well it has been able to  adapt not just to humans also to a host  of animal species and i think that's  something that maybe a lot of scientists  also underestimated in the beginning but  certainly everybody has always said you  know it takes two things for evolution  and one of them is that these mutations  have to come up in the first place which  is essentially chance so the more  infections you have the more likely you  are to get any any evolution at all and  then the second part is an evolutionary  pressure now as this virus sweeps  through humanity and immunity builds up  and we vaccinate people which also  builds up immunity of course the  evolutionary pressure is there so all we  can do at the moment really is to reduce  the number of infections globally and  cut off these these ways for the virus  to to evolve around our defenses but  we've been doing a very bad job of that  i'm afraid and as long as that continues  the chances are that the virus is also  going to continue to evolve and find new  ways to make trouble  all right kai schmidt many thanks for  for your time  earlier we spoke to the world health  organization spokesperson chris